  "plain": "In the 1950s, a wealthy man named Roger Babson is said to have set up a Gravity Research Foundation in New Boston, New Hampshire, to find a way to defeat gravity. The researcher Eric Weinstein describes it and repeats the usual reason given for it: that Babson's sister had drowned in a pond. He calls that story \"not really very plausible,\" which hints that something else lay behind it. Michels, telling it in his own words, calls Babson \"a patron of science\" who \"was obsessed with finding the solution to antigravity,\" and he leaves out both the town and the drowned sister. Weinstein's doubt matters because, if the cover story is false, the foundation might have served a hidden programme."
